<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>QaFramework.Api</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Generic HTTP plumbing and response assertions. Knows nothing about the trading domain -
    no endpoint paths, no DTOs, no business rules. Those live in TradingDemo.AppModel.
    That boundary is what would let this project be reused unchanged against a second product.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="RestSharp" />
    <PackageReference Include="JsonSchema.Net" />
    <PackageReference Include="AwesomeAssertions" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\QaFramework.Core\QaFramework.Core.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Two members are internal by design and are the subject of unit tests in tests/Framework.Tests:

      ApiRequestBuilder.Build(JsonSerializerOptions)  - assembles the RestRequest. Internal
          because RestSharp must not leak past ApiClient; making it public to test it would
          undo the layering the type exists to enforce.
      ApiResponse<T>'s constructor                    - only ApiClient may construct a response,
          so that a test cannot fabricate one and assert against a body no server ever sent.

    Both behaviours are worth testing directly: null-dropping and invariant number formatting
    are cross-machine bug classes, and lazy non-throwing deserialisation is what every
    negative-path test depends on. The alternatives were to widen the API surface permanently,
    or to drive every case through a real HTTP listener - which would test the transport
    stack again rather than the logic in question, and add a socket to a unit test suite.
    Granting one test assembly visibility is the smaller compromise, and it is visible here.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Framework.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
